A motion command sent right after an abort must run, not be dropped.

An abort lets the trajectory planner decelerate along the path before it
clears its queue, and while it does it refuses new segments ("TP is
aborting"). A client that aborts and at once sends the next MDI move used to
lose that move: the sequencer dispatched it into the decelerating planner and
the move was dropped with only an operator message. abort/g64 failed on CI
this way ("G64 redo": its MDI to the next Z level was dropped, the run started
from where the previous abort had left the machine).

The axes are limited to 1 in/s^2, of which the planner uses half along a
path (the rest is kept for blends). Aborted past X10 at ~3.2 in/s, the stop
takes over 6 s: the MDI always lands inside the deceleration, and the test
checks that it did. It
then requires the move to arrive and no "TP is aborting" message.
